@.str.74 = private unnamed_addr constant [4540 x i8] c"\0A\0A# Examples\0A\0A* Start a shell providing `youtube-dl` from the `nixpkgs` flake:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix shell nixpkgs#youtube-dl\0A  # youtube-dl --version\0A  2020.11.01.1\0A  ```\0A\0A* Start a shell providing GNU Hello from NixOS 20.03:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix shell nixpkgs/nixos-20.03#hello\0A  ```\0A\0A* Run GNU Hello:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix shell nixpkgs#hello --command hello --greeting 'Hi everybody!'\0A  Hi everybody!\0A  ```\0A\0A* Run multiple commands in a shell environment:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix shell nixpkgs#gnumake --command sh -c \22cd src && make\22\0A  ```\0A\0A* Run GNU Hello in a chroot store:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix shell --store ~/my-nix nixpkgs#hello --command hello\0A  ```\0A\0A* Start a shell providing GNU Hello in a chroot store:\0A\0A  ```console\0A  # nix shell --store ~/my-nix nixpkgs#hello nixpkgs#bashInteractive --command bash\0A  ```\0A\0A  Note that it's necessary to specify `bash` explicitly because your\0A  default shell (e.g. `/bin/bash`) generally will not exist in the\0A  chroot.\0A\0A# Description\0A\0A`nix shell` runs a command in an environment in which the `$PATH` variable\0Aprovides the specified [*installables*](./nix.md#installable). If no command is specified, it starts the\0Adefault shell of your user account specified by `$SHELL`.\0A\0A# Use as a `#!`-interpreter\0A\0AYou can use `nix` as a script interpreter to allow scripts written\0Ain arbitrary languages to obtain their own dependencies via Nix. This is\0Adone by starting the script with the following lines:\0A\0A```bash\0A#! /usr/bin/env nix\0A#! nix shell installables --command real-interpreter\0A```\0A\0Awhere *real-interpreter* is the \E2\80\9Creal\E2\80\9D script interpreter that will be\0Ainvoked by `nix shell` after it has obtained the dependencies and\0Ainitialised the environment, and *installables* are the attribute names of\0Athe dependencies in Nixpkgs.\0A\0AThe lines starting with `#! nix` specify options (see above). Note that you\0Acannot write `#! /usr/bin/env nix shell -i ...` because many operating systems\0Aonly allow one argument in `#!` lines.\0A\0AFor example, here is a Python script that depends on Python and the\0A`prettytable` package:\0A\0A```python\0A#! /usr/bin/env nix\0A#! nix shell github:tomberek/-#python3With.prettytable --command python\0A\0Aimport prettytable\0A\0A# Print a simple table.\0At = prettytable.PrettyTable([\22N\22, \22N^2\22])\0Afor n in range(1, 10): t.add_row([n, n * n])\0Aprint t\0A```\0A\0ASimilarly, the following is a Perl script that specifies that it\0Arequires Perl and the `HTML::TokeParser::Simple` and `LWP` packages:\0A\0A```perl\0A#! /usr/bin/env nix\0A#! nix shell github:tomberek/-#perlWith.HTMLTokeParserSimple.LWP --command perl -x\0A\0Ause HTML::TokeParser::Simple;\0A\0A# Fetch nixos.org and print all hrefs.\0Amy $p = HTML::TokeParser::Simple->new(url => 'http://nixos.org/');\0A\0Awhile (my $token = $p->get_tag(\22a\22)) {\0A    my $href = $token->get_attr(\22href\22);\0A    print \22$href\\n\22 if $href;\0A}\0A```\0A\0ASometimes you need to pass a simple Nix expression to customize a\0Apackage like Terraform:\0A\0A```bash\0A#! /usr/bin/env nix\0A#! nix shell --impure --expr ``\0A#! nix with (import (builtins.getFlake ''nixpkgs'') {});\0A#! nix terraform.withPlugins (plugins: [ plugins.openstack ])\0A#! nix ``\0A#! nix --command bash\0A\0Aterraform \22$@\22\0A```\0A\0A> **Note**\0A>\0A> You must use double backticks (```` `` ````) when passing a simple Nix expression\0A> in a nix shell shebang.\0A\0AFinally, using the merging of multiple nix shell shebangs the following\0AHaskell script uses a specific branch of Nixpkgs/NixOS (the 21.11 stable\0Abranch):\0A\0A```haskell\0A#!/usr/bin/env nix\0A#!nix shell --override-input nixpkgs github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixos-21.11\0A#!nix github:tomberek/-#haskellWith.download-curl.tagsoup --command runghc\0A\0Aimport Network.Curl.Download\0Aimport Text.HTML.TagSoup\0Aimport Data.Either\0Aimport Data.ByteString.Char8 (unpack)\0A\0A-- Fetch nixos.org and print all hrefs.\0Amain = do\0A  resp <- openURI \22https://nixos.org/\22\0A  let tags = filter (isTagOpenName \22a\22) $ parseTags $ unpack $ fromRight undefined resp\0A  let tags' = map (fromAttrib \22href\22) tags\0A  mapM_ putStrLn $ filter (/= \22\22) tags'\0A```\0A\0AIf you want to be even more precise, you can specify a specific revision\0Aof Nixpkgs:\0A\0A    #!nix shell --override-input nixpkgs github:NixOS/nixpkgs/eabc38219184cc3e04a974fe31857d8e0eac098d\0A\0AYou can also use a Nix expression to build your own dependencies. For example,\0Athe Python example could have been written as:\0A\0A```python\0A#! /usr/bin/env nix\0A#! nix shell --impure --file deps.nix -i python\0A```\0A\0Awhere the file `deps.nix` in the same directory as the `#!`-script\0Acontains:\0A\0A```nix\0Awith import <nixpkgs> {};\0Apython3.withPackages (ps: with ps; [ prettytable ])\0A```\0A\0A\0A\00", align 1
